#{[17592186511466 :question/worked-solution "The local extrema of $f$ occur at its critical points. A point $a$ in the domain of $f$ is a critical point of $f$ if ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}$ (for stationary points) or ${f}^{′}(a)$ doesn't exist.\n\nThe second partial derivative test states that if $a\\in {R}^{2}$ is a stationary point of $f(x,y)$ with Hessian determinant $H(a),$ we have the following:\n\n- If $H(a)>0,$ then:\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a local maximum at $a$\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)>0,$ then $f$ has a local minimum at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a saddle point at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)=0,$ then the second partial derivatives test is inconclusive.\n\nFirst, notice that since $$\n\\displaystyle {f}^{′}(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{x}({x}^{2}+2x-{y}^{2}) & -2y{e}^{x}\\end{bmatrix},\n$$ we must have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}={e}^{x}({x}^{2}+2x-{y}^{2}),\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=-2y{e}^{x}.\n$$\n\nThe first derivative exists for any $(x,y)$ in the domain of the function, so we only need to find the stationary points. Setting ${f}^{′}(x,y)={0}^{T},$ we obtain the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x}({x}^{2}+2x-{y}^{2}) &=0 \\\\ -2y{e}^{x} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSolving the second equation for $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n-2y{e}^{x}=0\\,⟹\\,y=0.\n$$\n\nThen, substituting this into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x}({x}^{2}+2x-{y}^{2}) & =0 \\\\ {e}^{x}({x}^{2}+2x-{0}^{2}) & =0 \\\\ {e}^{x}({x}^{2}+2x) & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}+2x & =0 \\\\ x(x+2) & =0 \\\\ x & =-2,0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $(0,0)$ and $(-2,0)$ are stationary points of $f(x,y).$\n\nTo classify these stationary points, we use the second partial derivative test.\n\nFirst, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({e}^{x}({x}^{2}+2x-{y}^{2}))={e}^{x}({x}^{2}+4x-{y}^{2}+2)\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-2y{e}^{x})=-2y{e}^{x} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({e}^{x}({x}^{2}+2x-{y}^{2}))=-2y{e}^{x}\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-2y{e}^{x})=-2{e}^{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second derivative is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x,y) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{x}({x}^{2}+4x-{y}^{2}+2) & -2y{e}^{x} \\\\ -2y{e}^{x} & -2{e}^{x}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the second derivative at each of the stationary points and use it to categorize them:\n\n- Evaluating the second derivative at $(0,0),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(0,0)=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the determinant $H(0,0),$ we get\n\n$$\nH(0,0)=det({f}^{″}(0,0))=2\\cdot (-2)-{0}^{2}=-4<0,\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(0,0)$ is a saddle point.\n\n- Evaluating the second derivative at $(-2,0),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(-2,0)=\\begin{bmatrix}-2{e}^{-2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2{e}^{-2}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the determinant $H(-2,0),$ we get\n\n$$\nH(-2,0)=det({f}^{″}(-2,0))=(-2{e}^{-2})^{2}-{0}^{2}=4{e}^{-4}>0\n$$\n\nFinally, since $H(-2,0)>0$ and $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(-2,0)=({f}^{″}(-2,0))_{11}=-2{e}^{-2}<0,$ the function $f$ has a local maximum at $(-2,0).$\n\nIn conclusion, $f(x,y)$ has a saddle point at $(0,0)$ and a local maximum at $(-2,0).$"] [17592186511470 :question/problem "Given that \n$$\n\\displaystyle {f}^{′}(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}-{e}^{y}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & {e}^{y}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\end{bmatrix},\n$$ \nevaluate the Hessian determinant of $f(x,y)$ at the point $(0,0).$"] [17592186511470 :question/worked-solution "For a function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R,$ given by\n\n$$\nf:\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{bmatrix}}_{x}⟶f({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}),\n$$\n\nthe second derivative of $f$ is given by the $n\\times n$ matrix\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}^{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThe Hessian determinant $H(x,y)$ is the determinant of the second derivative:\n\n$$\nH(x)=det({f}^{″}(x))\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that since $$\n\\displaystyle {f}^{′}(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}-{e}^{y}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & {e}^{y}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\end{bmatrix},\n$$ we must have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=-{e}^{y}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}={e}^{y}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-{e}^{y}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)=-{e}^{y}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({e}^{y}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)=-{e}^{y}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-{e}^{y}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)=-{e}^{y}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({e}^{y}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)={e}^{y}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x,y) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-{e}^{y}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & -{e}^{y}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ -{e}^{y}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & {e}^{y}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the second derivative at $(0,0),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(0,0)=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\nH(0,0)=(-1)\\cdot 1-{0}^{2}=-1.\n$$"] [17592186511476 :question/problem "Let $$\n\\displaystyle u=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 7 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix},v=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$ and $$\n\\displaystyle w=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$ be vectors such that $u\\,⊥\\,v.$ Find $(u-2v)\\cdot (v+2w).$"] [17592186511479 :question/problem "Let $$\n\\displaystyle u=\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ -1 \\\\ b \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},v=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix},\n$$ and $$\n\\displaystyle w=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$ be vectors such that $u$ is orthogonal to $v$ and $w.$ What is the value of $a-b?$"] [17592186511482 :question/worked-solution "The critical points of a multivariable function $f$ are the points $a$ in the domain of $f$ such that ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}$ or ${f}^{′}(a)$ does not exist, where\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(a)={\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{n}}\\end{bmatrix}|}_{x=a}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, the domain of $f$ is ${R}^{2},$ and the first partial derivatives of $f$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =(1-2{x}^{2}){e}^{-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =-2xy{e}^{-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, the first derivative of $f$ is\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}(1-2{x}^{2}){e}^{-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})} & -2xy{e}^{-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo find the critical points, we need to find the points $(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}$ such that ${f}^{′}(x,y)={0}^{T}$ or ${f}^{′}(x,y)$ does not exist. Note that, in our case, ${f}^{′}(x,y)$ exists everywhere, so we just need to find the points where ${f}^{′}(x,y)={0}^{T}.$\n\nSetting ${f}^{′}(x,y)={0}^{T},$ we have the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}(1-2{x}^{2}){e}^{-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})} &=0 \\\\ -2xy{e}^{-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSince ${e}^{-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})}\\ne 0$ for any point $(x,y)\\in {R}^{2},$ we must have \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}1-2{x}^{2} &=0 \\\\ -2xy &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSolving for $x$ in the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n1-2{x}^{2}=0\\,⟹\\,x=\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\n \n\nSubstituting these values into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-(\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})y=0\\,⟹\\,y=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our system has two solutions, $(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},0)$ and $(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},0).$\n\nIn conclusion, $f$ has two critical points, $(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},0)$ and $(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},0).$ Note that since ${f}^{′}(x)={0}^{T}$ at these two points, they are also both stationary points."] [17592186511486 :question/worked-solution "For a function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R,$ given by\n\n$$\nf:\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{bmatrix}}_{x}⟶f({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}),\n$$\n\nthe second derivative of $f$ is given by the $n\\times n$ matrix\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}^{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the first derivative of $f(x,y),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x,y) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & \\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x-y)) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x-y))\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x-y) & -\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x-y)\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x-y))=-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x-y)\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x-y))=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x-y) \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x-y))=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x-y)\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x-y))=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x-y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x,y) & =\\begin{bmatrix}-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x-y) & 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x-y) \\\\ 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x-y) & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x-y)\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate this matrix at $(0,-\\frac{\\pi }{2}).$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(0,-\\frac{\\pi }{2})=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186511494 :question/problem "Find the orthogonal projection matrix that maps $x\\in {R}^{2}$ onto the subspace spanned by an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =4$ of the matrix $$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186511494 :question/worked-solution "The spectral decomposition of a $2\\times 2$ symmetric matrix $A$ is given by\n\n$$\nA={\\lambda }_{1}{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{1}^{T}+{\\lambda }_{2}{u}_{2}\\cdot {u}_{2}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere ${u}_{1}$ and ${u}_{2}$ are orthonormal eigenvectors of $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}$ and ${\\lambda }_{2},$ respectively.\n\nMoreover, ${u}_{i}\\cdot {u}_{i}^{T}$ is the projection matrix corresponding to ${u}_{i}.$\n\nFirst, we need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to $\\lambda =4.$ Computing $A-4I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-4I & =\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 0\\end{bmatrix}-4\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-9 & 6 \\\\ 6 & -4\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{array}{cc|c}-9 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 6 & -4 & 0\\end{array}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+\\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{array}{cc|c}-9 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{array}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=\\frac{2}{3}{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{3}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting ${x}_{2}=3,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\nv=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nwhere $‖v‖=\\sqrt{13}.$ Dividing $v$ by its norm, we obtain the normalized eigenvector: \n\n$$\nu=\\frac{v}{‖v‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFinally, the required orthogonal projection matrix is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot {u}^{T} & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{13}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{13}\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 9\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186511495 :question/problem "Suppose that\n\n$\\,$ $$\n\\displaystyle {v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\\,\n$$ and $$\n\\displaystyle \\,{v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nare eigenvectors of a $2\\times 2$ symmetric matrix $A$ that correspond to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=3$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-2,$ respectively. Find the matrix $A.$"] [17592186511495 :question/worked-solution "The spectral decomposition of a $2\\times 2$ symmetric matrix $A$ is given by\n\n$$\nA={\\lambda }_{1}{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{1}^{T}+{\\lambda }_{2}{u}_{2}\\cdot {u}_{2}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere ${u}_{1}$ and ${u}_{2}$ are orthonormal eigenvectors of $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}$ and ${\\lambda }_{2},$ respectively.\n\nFirst, let's normalize our eigenvectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} & =\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {u}_{2} & =\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\lambda }_{1}{u}_{1}{u}_{1}^{T}+{\\lambda }_{2}{u}_{2}{u}_{2}^{T} \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}+(-2)\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\frac{1}{5}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}+(-2)\\cdot \\frac{1}{5}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(3\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}+(-2)\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}((3)\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}4 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}+(-2)\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\begin{bmatrix}10 & -10 \\\\ -10 & -5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -2 \\\\ -2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186511496 :question/worked-solution "The stationary points of a multivariable function $f$ are the points $a$ in the domain of $f$ such that ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T},$ where\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(a)={\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{n}}\\end{bmatrix}|}_{x=a}.\n$$\n\nIn tihs case, the domain of $f$ is ${R}^{2},$ and the first partial derivatives of $f$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =4x+4y \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =4x+2y+4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the first derivative of $f$ is\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}4x+4y & 4x+2y+4\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo find the stationary points, we need to find the points where ${f}^{′}(x,y)={0}^{T}.$\nSetting ${f}^{′}(x,y)={0}^{T},$ we have the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}4x+4y &=0 \\\\ 4x+2y+4 &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSolving for $x$ in the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n4x+4y=0\\,⟹\\,x=-y.\n$$\n\nThen, substituting $x=-y$ into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4(-y)+2y+4 & =0 \\\\ -4y+2y+4 & =0 \\\\ -2y & =-4 \\\\ y & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBack-substituting this value into $x=-y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\ny=2\\,⟹\\,x=-2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our system has one solution, $(-2,2).$\n\nIn conclusion, $f$ has one stationary point, $(-2,2).$"] [17592186511507 :question/problem "Consider the following vectors:\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,{a}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,{a}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $-3{a}_{1}+{a}_{2}+2{a}_{3}=0.$\n\nII. ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=0$ only if ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0.$\n\nIII. The set $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}\\}$ is linearly independent."] [17592186511507 :question/worked-solution "We say that three vectors ${v}_{1},{v}_{2}$ and ${v}_{3}$ are *linearly independent* if the only solution to the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{2}+{x}_{3}{v}_{3}=0\n$$\n\nis ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0.$ Otherwise, the vectors are *linearly dependent*.\n\nWith that in mind, let's look at each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Indeed:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3{a}_{1}+{a}_{2}+2{a}_{3} & =-3\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+2\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-12 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 10\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-12+0+2 \\\\ 3+6+4 \\\\ 0+0+10\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-10 \\\\ 13 \\\\ 10\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & \\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. The equation ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=0$ corresponds to the system\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}4{x}_{1}+{x}_{3} &=0 \\\\ -{x}_{1}+6{x}_{2}+2{x}_{3} &=0 \\\\ 5{x}_{3} &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNotice that we can reorder the equations in this system as follows:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{1}+6{x}_{2}+2{x}_{3} &=0 \\\\ 4{x}_{1}+{x}_{3} &=0 \\\\ 5{x}_{3} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSolving this system using back substitution, we find that the solution is ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statement II is true, the set $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}\\}$ is linearly independent.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""] [17592186511512 :question/problem "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,Σ=\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{6} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices $A$ and $Σ$ shown above. Given that $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ which of the following could be the matrix $V?$"] [17592186511512 :question/worked-solution "Recall that if  $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of a $3\\times 2$ matrix $A,$ then\n\n- $Σ$ is a $3\\times 2$ matrix with nonnegative elements on the main diagonal and zeroes outside the main diagonal,\n\n- $U$ is an orthogonal $3\\times 3$ matrix,\n\n- $V$ is an orthogonal $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nLet's find the singular value decomposition of $A$ using the usual algorithm.\n\n- **Step 1.** Compute ${A}^{T}\\,A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A & ={\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{T}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the singular values: From the matrix $Σ,$ we deduce that ${\\sigma }_{1}=\\sqrt{6}$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}=1.$\n\n- **Step 3.** Find the right singular vectors of $A$ (i.e., the unit eigenvectors of ${A}^{T}\\,A$):\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\sigma }_{1}^{2}=6,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A-6I & =\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{bmatrix}-6\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -4\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $({A}^{T}\\,A-6I)v=0$ gives the unit eigenvector\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}={\\sigma }_{2}^{2}=1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A-1I & =\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $({A}^{T}\\,A-1I)v=0$ gives the unit eigenvector\n\n$$\n{v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we construct the matrix $V:$\n\n$$\nV=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [17592186511514 :question/problem "$$\nU=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & 0 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,Σ=\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{10} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,V=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above. Given that $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ find the pseudoinverse ${A}^{+}.$"] [17592186511514 :question/worked-solution "If $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ then the pseudoinverse of $A$ is given by\n\n$$\nV\\,{Σ}^{+}\\,{U}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere ${Σ}^{+}$ is the pseudoinverse of $Σ.$\n\nFirst, notice that\n\n$$\nV=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{Σ}^{+}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,{U}^{T}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & \\sqrt{2}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{+} & =V\\,{Σ}^{+}\\,{U}^{T} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & \\sqrt{2}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & \\sqrt{2}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & \\sqrt{2}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186511526 :question/worked-solution "The critical points of a multivariable function $f$ are the points $a$ in the domain of $f$ such that ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}$ or ${f}^{′}(a)$ does not exist, where\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(a)={\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{n}}\\end{bmatrix}|}_{x=a}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, the domain of $f$ is ${R}^{2},$ and the first partial derivatives of $f$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & ={y}^{2}-4 \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =2xy+8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, the first derivative of $f$ is\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}{y}^{2}-4 & 2xy+8\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo find the critical points, we need to find the points $(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}$ such that ${f}^{′}(x,y)={0}^{T}$ or ${f}^{′}(x,y)$ does not exist. Note that, in our case, ${f}^{′}(x,y)$ exists everywhere, so we just need to find the points where ${f}^{′}(x,y)={0}^{T}.$\n\nSetting ${f}^{′}(x,y)={0}^{T},$ we have the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}-4 &=0 \\\\ 2xy+8 &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSolving for $x$ in the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n2xy+8=0\\,⟹\\,x=-\\frac{4}{y}.\n$$\n\nNow, solving for $y$ in the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}-4=0\\,⟹\\,y=\\pm 2.\n$$\n\nSubstituting these values into $x=-\\frac{4}{y},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y=-2 & \\,⟹\\,x=-\\frac{4}{(-2)}=2 \\\\ y=2 & \\,⟹\\,x=-\\frac{4}{2}=-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our system has two solutions, $(2,-2)$ and $(-2,2).$\n\nIn conclusion, $f$ has two critical points, $(2,-2)$ and $(-2,2).$ Note that since ${f}^{′}(x)={0}^{T}$ at these two points, they are also both stationary points."] [17592186511540 :question/problem "If $$\n\\displaystyle u=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 2k \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$ and $$\n\\displaystyle v=\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ k\\end{bmatrix}\n$$ are orthogonal vectors, then $k=$"] [17592186511545 :question/worked-solution "For a function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R,$ given by\n\n$$\nf:\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{bmatrix}}_{x}⟶f({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}),\n$$\n\nthe second derivative of $f$ is given by the $n\\times n$ matrix\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}^{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the first derivative of $f(x,y),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x,y) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & \\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y & {x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y)=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y)=2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y)=2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y)=-{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x,y) & =\\begin{bmatrix}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡y & 2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y \\\\ 2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y & -{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate this matrix at $(1,\\pi ).$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(1,\\pi )=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186511549 :question/worked-solution "The stationary points of a multivariable function $f$ are the points $a$ in the domain of $f$ such that ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T},$ where\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(a)={\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{n}}\\end{bmatrix}|}_{x=a}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, the domain of $f$ is ${R}^{2},$ and the first partial derivatives of $f$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =2x{e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =2y{e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the first derivative of $f$ is\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}2x{e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} & 2y{e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo find the stationary points, we need to find the points where ${f}^{′}(x,y)={0}^{T}.$\nSetting ${f}^{′}(x,y)={0}^{T},$ we have the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2x{e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} &=0 \\\\ 2y{e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSince ${e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\ne 0$ for any point $(x,y)\\in {R}^{2},$ we must have \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=0 \\\\ y &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our system has one solution, $(0,0).$\n\nIn conclusion, $f$ has one stationary point, $(0,0).$"] [17592186511564 :question/problem "Consider the **orthonormal** set $U$ below. By adding a new vector $x=[{x}_{1},\\,{x}_{2},\\,{x}_{3}]^{T},$ extend the set so that it forms an **orthonormal** basis of ${R}^{3}.$ What is the value of $|{x}_{1}|+|{x}_{2}|+|{x}_{3}|?$\n\n$$\nU=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{17}} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{17}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{17}}\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ 0 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}\\}\n$$"] [17592186511564 :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vectors of $U$ as ${u}_{1},\\,{u}_{2}.$ To extend the given set to an orthonormal basis of ${R}^{3}$, we need to find a third vector $x$ such that $‖x‖=1,$ and\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}\\cdot x &=0 \\\\ {u}_{2}\\cdot x &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{17}}{x}_{1}-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{17}}{x}_{2}+\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{17}}{x}_{3} &=0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}{x}_{1}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}{x}_{3} &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNow, we consider the augmented matrix $M$ of the system above and reduce it to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{17}} & -\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{17}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{17}} & 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & 0 & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & 0\\end{array}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}:=\\sqrt{17}\\cdot {R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{2}:=\\sqrt{2}\\cdot {R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}2 & -3 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -1 & 0\\end{array}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:=2{R}_{2}-{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}2 & -3 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -4 & 0\\end{array}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there are two pivot columns ($1$st and $2$nd). Thus, ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we get ${x}_{2}=\\frac{4}{3}{x}_{3},$ and from the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}={x}_{3}.$\n\nSo, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{3} \\\\ \\frac{4}{3}{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{3}=3,$ we obtain the vector \n$$\n\\displaystyle x=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$ \n\nNow, we need to normalize $x.$ Notice that $‖x‖=\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+{4}^{2}+{3}^{2}}=\\sqrt{34}.$ So, we normalize $x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{x}{‖x‖}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{34}} \\\\ \\frac{4}{\\sqrt{34}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{34}}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, an orthonormal basis of ${R}^{3}$ is\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{17}} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{17}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{17}}\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ 0 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{34}} \\\\ \\frac{4}{\\sqrt{34}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{34}}\\end{bmatrix}\\}.\n$$\n\nFinally, $$\n\\displaystyle |{x}_{1}|+|{x}_{2}|+|{x}_{3}|=|\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{34}}|+|\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{34}}|+|\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{34}}|=\\frac{10}{\\sqrt{34}}.\n$$"] [17592186511568 :question/problem "$$\n{U}_{r}=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{Σ}_{r}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix},\\,{V}_{r}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above. Given that $A={U}_{r}{Σ}_{r}{V}_{r}^{T}$ is a reduced singular value decomposition of $A,$ find the pseudoinverse ${A}^{+}.$"] [17592186511568 :question/worked-solution "If $A={U}_{r}{Σ}_{r}{V}_{r}^{T}$ is a reduced singular value decomposition of $A,$ then the pseudoinverse of $A$ is given by\n\n$$\n{V}_{r}\\,{Σ}_{r}^{+}\\,{U}_{r}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere ${Σ}_{r}^{+}$ is the pseudoinverse of ${Σ}_{r}.$\n\nFirst, notice that\n\n$$\n{V}_{r}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{Σ}_{r}^{+}=\\frac{1}{6}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix},\\,{U}_{r}^{T}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{+} & ={V}_{r}\\,{Σ}_{r}^{+}\\,{U}_{r}^{T} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\frac{1}{6}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 5 \\\\ 5 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186511585 :question/problem "Let $$\n\\displaystyle u=\\begin{bmatrix}7 \\\\ k \\\\ k \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$ and $$\n\\displaystyle v=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -k-1 \\\\ -7 \\\\ -12\\end{bmatrix}\n$$ be vectors such that  $u⊥v.$ Find the value of $k.$"] [17592186512118 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186512129 :answer/value "{H}^{⊥}=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{7}{8}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}\\,:\\,{x}_{2}\\in R\\}"] [17592186512130 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}30 & 15 \\\\ 15 & 26\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186512157 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 